# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the sanitizer CI jobs (TSAN_OPTIONS).
#
# Every entry is race_top: it hides a race only when the access itself is in
# that function, never because the function is further up a stack. The
# collector's own stack runs through GlobalAllocator::Collect and every Haxe
# thread through HaxeThread_obj::create, so a plain race: entry for either
# would hide everything. Tracked in openfl/libhxcpp#148.

# GC allocator: finding a block is lock-free by design. Reads of a block's
# mOwned and of mNextFreeBlockOfSize are hints; a block is claimed with a
# compare-and-swap on its mZeroLock and checked again under it.
race_top:GlobalAllocator::GetNextFree
race_top:GlobalAllocator::GetFreeBlock
race_top:GlobalAllocator::AllocMoreBlocks
race_top:GlobalAllocator::AllocLarge
race_top:GlobalAllocator::ThreadLoop

# GC: WaitForSafe, inlined into Collect, clears a running thread's allocation
# limits without a lock so that its next allocation fails and it parks.
race_top:GlobalAllocator::Collect

# GC: parallel marking. Mark threads set the same mark bytes, which only ever
# go one way, and hand chunks of work between them.
race_top:hx::MarkObjectAllocUnchecked
race_top:hx::MarkAllocUnchecked
race_top:hx::MarkObjectArray
race_top:hx::MarkContext::processMarkStack
race_top:hx::GlobalChunks::pushJob
race_top:wakeThreadLocked

# GC: a thread in a GC-free zone keeps running on the stack below where it
# entered, which the collector scans conservatively from there. What it reads
# below the frame that entered is dead, and can only keep extra objects alive.
# What the thread wrote before entering is ordered by mGCFreeZone.
race_top:hx::MarkConservative
# Entering a GC-free zone copies the caller's whole frame for that scan,
# including locals another thread may be writing through a reference.
race_top:hx::CaptureCallerFrame

# Haxe 4.3 std (sys.thread.Thread): HaxeThread.create stores the new thread's
# handle into an anonymous object from both the new thread and its creator.
race_top:sys::thread::_Thread::HaxeThread_obj::create
race_top:hx::Anon_obj::__Field
race_top:hx::Anon_obj::__SetField

# Strings cache their hash on first use without a lock: the value is written,
# a release fence published, then the flag bit set; readers check the bit and
# pair it with an acquire fence. Two threads hashing one string write the same
# value.
race_top:String::hash

# SQLite's start-up checks whether it is initialised without a lock first.
race:sqlite3Initialize
